;; Deprivation profile: a machine with no Node.js the application could borrow.
;;
;; A packaged Desktop application is supposed to be self-contained, and the only
;; honest way to test that claim is to take every other runtime away and see
;; whether it still serves a capability. A sanitized PATH alone does not do it —
;; the native host also falls back to three fixed package-manager locations, and
;; on a developer's machine at least one of them has a Node.
;;
;; So the deprivation is two things at once, and the test applies both:
;;   * `env -i ... PATH=/usr/bin`, which holds no `node`
;;   * this profile, which denies reading *and* executing under the three
;;     fallback roots the host hardcodes, plus /sw for the same reason.
;;
;; Reading is denied as well as executing because a resolver that can stat a
;; runtime it cannot run would report a different failure than the one this test
;; is about.
(version 1)
(allow default)
(deny file-read* file-write* file-map-executable process-exec*
  (subpath "/usr/local")
  (subpath "/opt/homebrew")
  (subpath "/opt/local")
  (subpath "/sw"))
